      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate the serum soluble CD40 ligand (sCD40L) levels in coronary heart disease and its relationship with MMP-9 and to explore the potential predicting factor for the acute coronary syndrome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay was used to measure the sCD40L and MMP-9 in 79 patients with coronary heart disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "sCD40L level was significantly higher in patients with acute coronary syndrome [(1.96 +/- 0.84) ng/ml and (2.23 +/- 0.99) ng/ml in unstable angina and acute myocardial infarction group, respectively] than in stable coronary heart disease patients [(1.20 +/- 0.76) ng/ml, P < 0.05]. Serum sCD40L was significantly correlated with MMP-9 (r = 0.401 , P < 0.01), and sCD40L level was significantly correlated with TG (r = 0.254, P = 0.039), HDL (r = -0.234, P = 0.031), and HDL-C (r = -0.253, P = 0.024)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Serum sCD40L and MMP-9 levels were elevated in acute coronary syndrome, suggesting the possible relation to the pathogenesis of ACS and it may serve as a potential marker of plaque stability."
        }
      ]},
